sections:
- intended use
- how to use
- available parameters
- warnings that can be raised by this map



intended use
------------

This map is intended for use with amide (~N(D)C(=O)~) (smiles notation) groups, specifically those that live in proteins, in the backbones. In other words, it looks for the amide units formed through linking amino acids.

The goal is to calculate the absorption frequency of the Amide I mode, where the C=O bond stretches.

The notation is not wrong - these maps assume in their frequency calculations that this is a deuterated group. If non-deuterated values are desired, the resulting spectrum should be blue-shifted by about 10 cm-1. (https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0064518)



how to use
----------

Be aware that the maps used in this overarching map have been designed to be used in combination with certain other maps, or with certain force fields. While a map should still work with a different forcefield, it is still highly recommended to use the intended/tested combinations. 
- In general, the OPLS-AA forcefield is recommended, except for the Tokmakoff map, which has been designed for the CHARMM-27 forcefield.
- The Jansen dipole map should only be used together with the Jansen frequency map (this is enforced by this map).
- It is common to also calculate couplings when working with amides. This should be done with one of the specially-designed amide coupling maps. See (https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jctc.6b00420) to find out what coupling map is best for your usecase.
- The peaks for these systems take up the same spectral space as sidechain amides, so to get better agreement with experiment, you'll probably want to use the sidechain map along this one.



Available parameters
--------------------

When using this map, the following parameters become available to use in any input parameter source (default file, input file, command line):

AmideBB.frequency_map_choice
    shorthand: AmideBB.fmc
    options: Tokmakoff, Skinner, Jansen, Cho, Hirst (default = Skinner)

    Indicate the program which frequency map should be used. Skinner is the default as it gives the best result for 2-dimensional IR absorption spectra, while being fairly cheap. It is also the only map which has a separately-developed map for sidechains, instead of using the backbone maps with a fixed shift.  (https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jctc.6b00420)

    Tokmakoff, Cho and Hirst maps are cheaper than Skinner, but still provide decent results. Jansen is more expensive than Skinner, but gives results closer to experiment when predicting linear IR spectra. (same reference)

AmideBB.dipole_map_choice
    shorthand: AmideBB.dmc
    options: Torii, Jansen (default = Torii)

    Indicates which dipole map should be used. Torii is the default as the Skinner frequency map assumes it, and is more general. Jansen should only be used when using the Jansen frequency map, too.

AmideBB.residue_order
    no shorthand
    options: resname, resnum (default = resnum)

    In what order the residues should be saved. This influences all output files (like hamiltonian, dipoles, etc). The difference is caused by the fact this map treats two different protein residues.

    resnum has been taken as the default, as this is the same order that AIM uses.

AmideBB.pos_choice
    no shorthand
    options: C, O, N, D, Torii (default = C)

    When saving the position of this group, what position should be taken. The first four options save the position of that atom, Torii saves the position at which the Torii dipoles are assumed. This is in between the CNO atoms: 0.077*C + 0.665*O + 0.258*N

AmideBB.dp1_choice
    no shorthand
    options: C, O, N, D, Torii (default = C)

    When saving the doublepos of this group, what position should be taken as first position. The first four options save the position of that atom, Torii saves the position at which the Torii dipoles are assumed. This is in between the CNO atoms: 0.077*C + 0.665*O + 0.258*N

AmideBB.dp2_choice
    no shorthand
    options: C, O, N, D, Torii (default = N)

    When saving the doublepos of this group, what position should be taken as second position. The first four options save the position of that atom, Torii saves the position at which the Torii dipoles are assumed. This is in between the CNO atoms: 0.077*C + 0.665*O + 0.258*N

AmideBB.allow_map_mismatch
    no shorthand
    options: true, false (default = false)

    When using this map in conjunction with the AmideSC map (developed for protein sidechain amides), it makes most physical sense to use the same frequency and dipole methods for both maps. By default, this map forces users to use the same methods for both maps (in order to avoid user error). If it is truly intended to have different methods active, this parameter can be used to have the map allow that.


warnings that can be raised by this map
---------------------------------------

map_AmideBB_1
    This error is raised when the Jansen dipole map is requested without the
    Jansen frequency map. The Jansen dipole map may be used if and only if the Jansen frequency map is used in the same run.

    This has to do with the fact that both maps require knowledge of the electrostatic gradient, and no other frequency map does. If any other frequency map is used, no gradient data will be collected, so results from this map will be wrong.

    Another reason is the fact that the two maps have been developed together with the intention of them working together. That means that to obtain the best results with either one, the other is needed, too.

map_AmideBB_2
    This error is raised when the Jansen dipole map is requested without the
    Jansen frequency map. The Jansen dipole map may be used if and only if the Jansen frequency map is used in the same run.

    This has to do with the fact that both maps require knowledge of the electrostatic gradient, and no other frequency map does. If any other frequency map is used, no gradient data will be collected, so results from this map will be wrong.

    Another reason is the fact that the two maps have been developed together with the intention of them working together. That means that to obtain the best results with either one, the other is needed, too.

